Only two points here:

1.  Your statement about "others may have had a different reaction" is ,
of course, true -- and others have, indeed, had a different reaction in
the past.  We could trade anecdotal evidence forever, though, and get
nowhere; the point being made here isn't about the past, it's about the
future.

We can't know for sure what any given person will think of the game
except from what they tell us; we only know that among new players, some
couldn't care less (like yourself), others point at it and say "That's
stupid, you didn't even fix that?", and still others leap on the flaw
and start beating it to death with both hands.  I would prefer to not
have new players come in and see flagrant design flaws smack in the
middle of the game; thus, I would prefer to fix the abuse.

2.  Your last sentence really is very telling about what pudding farming
is, and why it needs to be fixed in the first place.  When the capacity
exists to obtain infinite items, the game itself reduced from
multi-dimensional to one-dimensional, and this is _never_ good.  A
well-designed game should be about choosing between balanced options,
and the possibility to "just have it all" really removes all the choices
from the game by rendering them moot.

(Take, for example, the circumstance surrounding body armor.  Barring
those few people who intentional play extra-goofy conducts, there are
only two types of body armor people generally ascend with:  gray and
silver dragonscale mail.  Why?  Because dragonscale mail allows you to
"have it all" -- high AC, low weight, and no spellcasting hindrance.
Gray and silver happen to contain one each of the currently-mandatory
intrinsics; magic resistance and reflection.  Thus, almost all players
choose to "have it all" in a single package, and despite all the other
types of body armor available in the game, the "choice" that should
normally result in significant variety really ends up being only a coin
flip... gray or silver?)

